I want to change how rendering works.

No longer going to have a 'Driver' that feeds all renderers a stream of events.

Each Renderer should be its own object with a process, and should require a log as a source/attribute. It can be pointed at a live log dir, an extracted log dir, a .yath log file (tarball or sqlite) or at a log in a database.

The renderer base class or role (whichever is easier to work with or implement) will implement the essential functionality of looping over the log file, and in the case of a live log watching for changes.

Renderer life-cycle in the parent process:

 * renderer constructor insures we have a logfile/dir/db we can work with, and verifies other settings are correct (these will vary from renderer to renderer, but optionslike verbose and quiet, etc).
 * renderer->start() # creates a child process to do the rendering, fork+exec on linux, system(1, ...) trick for windows.
 * renderer->finish() # Parent process calls it to send an IPC message to the renderer indicating the run is complete (on live runs this happens when the runner is done, on non-live runs this is called right away to indicate it is not live). This will also wait() on the pid of the child.

Renderer life-cycle in the child process:

 * Reconstruct from construction arguments, since fork+exec or system(1) means fresh memory, probably from a json file.
 * Open the log
 * Anything else renderer-specific for starting up
 * Render Loop
    * Ask log for producers we have not seen yet (have not seen any on first loop)
    - (producer is anything that produces artifacts, tets jobs, runs, services, etc)
    * Logger decides which producers matter to it
    * Logger asks for the relevent artifacts, following example is for terminal renderer:
        * Sees a test job produce, asks for spec
            * Writes test job started output message
        * Asks for 'report',
            * if not found try again in future loop (live only)
            * If found (the next depends on verbosity, but assume qvf for this example)
             * Read report, if pass write job passed message and nothing else
             * If report fail, write jobs event.jsonl events to terminal (formatteed) then write job failed line.
             * Close out job internally so we do not ask about it on future loops

Instead of events being fed to the renderers in a stream, the renderers ask for what they care about.

We will also introduce the concept of a formatter

A formatter converts an artifacts (like events.jsonl) into an alternate form, typically for display purposes.

Example formatters:

 * txt      - Convert artifacts to human readable text (like the current Default renderer, but with no ANSI escape sequences)
 * tty      - Same as 'text' but with ANSI escape sequences and color, text and tty should share most underlying code.
 * xml      - For use in the JUnit renderer (if it makes sense, it ma not make sense to use a formatter for junit)
 * csv      - do not implement, just an example)
 * html     - do not implement this yet, will be used for Server renderer

Formatter name also indicates filename extension, when an artifact is converted
from .jsonl to .html we will want to be able to cache the new .html artifact
for all log formats except tarballs (because it is hard to append to our
tarballs). When we convert an artifact via a formatter, the new format should
be added to the log (directory, db, etc). Caching should only occur when
converting a completed artifact, not when converting a partial 'events.jsonl'.

Also some things we currently call renderers will need to move to a new
category, Suggest a name for this category. It is things like the current
Summary and Notifier renderers which only act at the end of a run to do final
actions, and do not need a child process to render every producer and event.

I want to make a very clean and clear distinction for these roles:
 * Renderer - Reads from producers and "renders" them for something else to consume them
 * Formatter - Takes artifacts and converts them to other formats
 * ??? - Produces final output or takes final actions (triggers?) when a run is complete

We are not going to implement/Convert the Server renderer at this time

